{"query": {"id": "20260628161330515741_d4c57404", "handle": "q1", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” archaeological gold ring Pylos discovery excavations", "importance": 
"core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628161529395545_9ac8e466", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Evans introduces a \"sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and 
bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" and identifies among them a large gold 
signet-ring that he names the \"Ring of Nestor,\" describing it as the most 
important piece of the find.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526068537_afd24e74"], 
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628161529551110_2cf65334", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The article states that the treasure, including the Ring of Nestor, was
discovered in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near Thisbê by a local 
peasant at a place called Dombrena, during wartime conditions that prevented 
normal archaeological oversight.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526068537_afd24e74"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628161529665140_2d7c2255", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans describes 
the Ring of Nestor as a large gold signet-ring with a bezel bearing elaborate 
figured scenes, and he interprets these scenes as providing a \"glimpse into the
Minoan after-world.\"", "source_ids": ["20260628161526068537_afd24e74"], 
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628161529753692_9431c8a3", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "In situating the ring chronologically, Evans argues that it belongs to 
Late Helladic or Late Mycenaean times but displays pronounced Minoan stylistic 
elements, particularly in its glyptic style and religious imagery.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628161526068537_afd24e74"], "citations": [1]}, {"id": 
"20260628161529846189_4277c37f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The JHS article 
describes the find in the rock tomb as a \"truly royal treasure\" and notes that
the gold rings and bead seals, including the Ring of Nestor, passed through 
private hands before Evans was able to examine and document them.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628161526116364_0b859111"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628161529940143_3d449563", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans provides a 
technical description of the Ring of Nestor as constructed from gold sheet, with
a large bezel attached to a hoop, and describes multiple scenes on the bezel 
featuring anthropomorphic figures, animals, architectural structures, and 
landscape or celestial elements.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526116364_0b859111"], "citations": [2]}, {"id": 
"20260628161530038135_0a7dfb7f", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Heidelberg 
edition’s table of contents lists section I as \"Sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" and section II as \"A glimpse
into the Minoan after-world,\" followed by plates I–V illustrating the Ring of 
Nestor and related objects.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526151885_5767b64e"], 
"citations": [3]}, {"id": "20260628161530136278_32390f2f", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Within the first section, Evans catalogs the rings and bead seals from 
the Thisbe tomb, giving physical measurements and manufacturing notes, and 
identifies the Ring of Nestor as distinguished by its unusually large size and 
intricate glyptic decoration compared to the other pieces.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526151885_5767b64e"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628161530237008_4df81378", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The second section
of Evans’s study interprets the ring’s imagery as showing processional and 
ritual scenes with possible deities and worshippers, alongside symbolic motifs 
such as trees, altars, and celestial elements, which he takes to reflect 
conceptions of the afterlife.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526151885_5767b64e"],
"citations": [3]}, {"id": "20260628161530324750_376a8d75", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The HathiTrust record specifies the title \"ʻThe ring of Nestor;̓ a 
glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia\" and notes that the 
publication includes five plates.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526185536_e9f11b73"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628161530413908_3fda67d3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "J.L. Davis writes 
that before the Griffin Warrior discovery, the best-known large, elaborately 
engraved gold signet ring associated with the Pylos area was the \"Ring of 
Nestor,\" which had long served as a key comparative piece in discussions of 
Mycenaean gold rings.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526223570_993ff599"], 
"citations": [5]}, {"id": "20260628161530510081_6be441df", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The article notes that Arthur Evans acquired the Ring of Nestor from 
local sources rather than as part of a controlled excavation, and states that as
a result its archaeological credentials and precise provenance are debated among
scholars.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526223570_993ff599"], "citations": [5]}, 
{"id": "20260628161530612021_aa1dd9f3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Davis 
indicates that the Ring of Nestor may not have originated from the Palace of 
Nestor excavations themselves, suggesting that earlier assumptions linking it 
directly to that site should be treated with caution.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526223570_993ff599"], "citations": [5]}, {"id": 
"20260628161530698127_02d6c37d", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In \"The Lord of 
the Gold Rings,\" Davis et al. describe the Ring of Nestor as a celebrated gold 
signet ring with elaborate iconography and remark that its band and bezel are 
technically and stylistically comparable to rings from Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos 
and to the Ring of Minos.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526260710_56437a35"], 
"citations": [6]}, {"id": "20260628161530785773_c7f64550", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The authors use these comparisons to argue for placing the Ring of 
Nestor within a particular phase of Mycenaean glyptic development and to suggest
that it may share workshop traditions with rings from securely excavated Pylian 
tombs.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526260710_56437a35"], "citations": [6]}, 
{"id": "20260628161530886294_609aaccd", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Davis and 
colleagues state that because the Ring of Nestor lacks secure excavation context
and has a problematic acquisition history, it should be treated with caution and
not relied upon as primary evidence for detailed reconstructions of Pylian 
archaeological contexts.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526260710_56437a35"], 
"citations": [6]}, {"id": "20260628161530992486_98f0acb2", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The related Academia.edu version of the study notes that \"Both the 
Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos have bands strikingly similar\" to certain 
rings from excavations at the Palace of Nestor, Pylos.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526299320_cea5570f"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628161531084279_ba9d1847", "type": "Evidence", "text": "This version 
emphasizes that objects like the Ring of Nestor, which were acquired under early
20th-century conditions without secure documentation, must be integrated into 
archaeological syntheses only with explicit acknowledgment of their uncertain 
provenance.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526299320_cea5570f"], "citations": 
