{"query": {"id": "20260628161330515741_d4c57404", "handle": "q1", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” archaeological gold ring Pylos discovery excavations", "importance": 
"core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628161531368310_53ba0af5", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Hudler recommends that scholars explicitly label objects like the Ring 
of Nestor as problematic and limit their role in reconstructions of specific 
historical or ritual scenarios, suggesting instead that they be used cautiously 
and primarily in broader stylistic or technological discussions.", "source_ids":
["20260628161526340237_391a31d0"], "citations": [8]}, {"id": 
"20260628161531462171_a12e07ef", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The technological 
study of gold objects from four tholos tombs at Pylos lists Arthur Evans’s \"The
Ring of Nestor: A glimpse into the Minoan after-world\" and J.L. Davis et al.’s 
\"The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos\" among its 
references when discussing Mycenaean gold rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628161531549810_f1b1fe79", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In comparing 
production techniques and alloy compositions of gold rings from Pylian tholos 
tombs to famous examples, the authors mention the Ring of Nestor alongside the 
Griffin Warrior rings, using them to discuss possible shared workshop traditions
and technological parallels while noting the Ring of Nestor’s uncertain 
provenance.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": 
[9]}, {"id": "20260628161531645508_83891154", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
technological study explicitly cautions that, because the Ring of Nestor does 
not come from a securely excavated context, it should not be used on an equal 
footing with firmly provenanced Pylian finds when drawing conclusions about 
local production and deposition practices.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": [9]}, {"id": 
"20260628161603920036_31993129", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor is
a large gold signet ring, constructed from gold sheet with a hoop and a large 
bezel bearing multiple elaborate figured scenes, including anthropomorphic 
figures, animals, architectural structures, and natural or celestial motifs.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628161526068537_afd24e74", "20260628161526116364_0b859111",
"20260628161526151885_5767b64e"], "citations": [1, 2, 3]}, {"id": 
"20260628161604156840_57ab1457", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur J. Evans 
introduced and named the Ring of Nestor as the most important piece of a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly found in a 
Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near Thisbê, in Boeotia.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526068537_afd24e74", "20260628161526116364_0b859111"], "citations":
[1, 2]}, {"id": "20260628161604346915_c9bbc56f", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"According to Arthur Evans’s account, the treasure that included the Ring of 
Nestor was discovered in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock-cut tomb near Thisbê at a 
place called Dombrena by a local peasant, and the objects passed through private
hands before Evans examined them.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161526068537_afd24e74", "20260628161526116364_0b859111"], "citations":
[1, 2]}, {"id": "20260628161604502700_ce23022c", "type": "Claim", "text": "The 
Ring of Nestor lacks secure excavation records and was acquired by Arthur Evans 
from local sources rather than through controlled archaeological excavation, 
leaving its precise provenance and archaeological credentials debated and 
uncertain.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526223570_993ff599", 
"20260628161526260710_56437a35", "20260628161526299320_cea5570f", 
"20260628161526340237_391a31d0", "20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": 
[5, 6, 7, 8, 9]}, {"id": "20260628161604815914_3596c2db", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "Modern scholars such as Cathrin Hudler and J.L. Davis classify the Ring
of Nestor as a problematic or \"gemma dubitanda\" object whose unclear 
provenance undermines its reliability for precise arguments about Mycenaean 
chronology, function, religious beliefs, or specific Pylian archaeological 
contexts.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526260710_56437a35", 
"20260628161526340237_391a31d0", "20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": 
[6, 8, 9]}, {"id": "20260628161605112909_b8b37b5a", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Despite its uncertain provenance, the Ring of Nestor is widely treated in 
scholarship as a celebrated masterpiece of Aegean gold glyptic and has long 
played a prominent comparative role in studies of Mycenaean gold rings.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628161526223570_993ff599", 
"20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": [5, 9]}, {"id": 
"20260628161605316482_2db8a019", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur Evans dates 
the Ring of Nestor to the Late Helladic or Late Mycenaean period and argues that
its glyptic style and religious imagery show strong Minoan stylistic 
influence.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526068537_afd24e74", 
"20260628161526116364_0b859111"], "citations": [1, 2]}, {"id": 
"20260628161605441840_8abe22fc", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans interprets the 
Ring of Nestor’s iconography—processional and ritual scenes with possible 
deities and worshippers, trees, altars, and celestial motifs—as offering a 
vision or \"glimpse\" of a Minoan-style afterworld and reflecting beliefs about 
the afterlife.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526068537_afd24e74", 
"20260628161526116364_0b859111", "20260628161526151885_5767b64e", 
"20260628161526185536_e9f11b73"], "citations": [1, 2, 3, 4]}, {"id": 
"20260628161605694745_97df87f2", "type": "Claim", "text": "Comparative stylistic
and technical studies by Davis and colleagues note that the band and bezel of 
the Ring of Nestor are strikingly similar to rings from Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos 
and to the so-called Ring of Minos, and use these similarities to suggest a 
particular phase of Mycenaean glyptic development and possible shared workshop 
traditions.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526260710_56437a35", 
"20260628161526299320_cea5570f", "20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": 
[6, 7, 9]}, {"id": "20260628161605958293_70e678ec", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Recent discussions question earlier assumptions that the Ring of Nestor 
originated from the Palace of Nestor excavations at Pylos, emphasizing that it 
may not come from that site and therefore should not be equated with securely 
excavated Pylian assemblages.", "source_ids": ["20260628161526223570_993ff599", 
"20260628161526384198_063ab3c8"], "citations": [5, 9]}], "offset": 20, "limit": 
20, "total": 34, "next_offset": null}
