submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': '', 
'section_type': 'lead', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628161531756178_91ae4f62', '20260628161537039428_0e9a8710', 
'20260628161537144810_9aa50af4'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is a 
large gold signet ring attributed to the Aegean Bronze Age and widely discussed 
as a notable example of Mycenaean and Minoan-style glyptic art.[1][16] Arthur J.
Evans introduced the name "Ring of Nestor" for what he considered the most 
important piece in a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals, 
and he interpreted its densely populated figural scenes as offering a "glimpse" 
into a Minoan conception of the afterlife.[1][3][4] Modern museum and scholarly 
catalogues generally assign the ring to the Late Minoan I–IB–II period, 
proposing an absolute date in the range c. 1600–1400 BC, although opinions 
differ regarding its precise chronology and authenticity.[10][15][16] Despite 
uncertainty surrounding its provenance and debates over whether it is genuinely 
ancient or a modern forgery, the Ring of Nestor has long been treated as a 
celebrated and influential piece in studies of Aegean iconography and gold ring 
technology.[5][9][11][12]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526151885_5767b64e', 
'20260628161526185536_e9f11b73', '20260628161533656509_d7512d4b', 
'20260628161533913859_f729e462', '20260628161533948547_acd64085', 
'20260628161526223570_993ff599', '20260628161526384198_063ab3c8', 
'20260628161533760498_43401e75', '20260628161533793604_6869b567']}]}, {'title': 
'Discovery and provenance', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids':
['20260628161531756178_91ae4f62', '20260628161537144810_9aa50af4'], 'blocks': 
[{'text': 'Arthur J. Evans first published the Ring of Nestor in 1925 in a study
titled "The Ring of Nestor; a glimpse into the Minoan after-world, and a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia," 
in which he introduced and named the ring and placed it at the centre of his 
discussion.[1][3][4] Evans presented the ring as the most important item within 
a group of gold signet rings and bead seals that he described as a sepulchral 
treasure from a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near the town of Thisbê in 
Boeotia.[1][3]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526116364_0b859111', 
'20260628161526151885_5767b64e', '20260628161526185536_e9f11b73']}, {'text': 
'According to Evans’s account, the treasure that included the Ring of Nestor was
discovered in 1915 by a local peasant at a place called Dombrena, near Thisbê, 
during wartime conditions that prevented normal archaeological oversight.[1][2] 
Evans wrote that the objects, including the ring, passed through private hands 
before he was able to examine and document them, emphasising that they did not 
come from a controlled excavation and were acquired under the informal 
collecting practices of the early twentieth century.[2][3]', 'kind': 
'paragraph', 'source_ids': ['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', 
'20260628161526116364_0b859111']}, {'text': 'Later scholarship has drawn 
attention to the problematic nature of this provenance and has questioned the 
reliability of Evans’s association of the ring with the Thisbê tomb, as the 
original find circumstances were not recorded by professional 
archaeologists.[5][6][8] Researchers such as J.L. Davis and Cathrin Hudler 
classify the Ring of Nestor as a "gemma dubitanda" or problematic object, noting
that its archaeological credentials are debated and that its context cannot be 
securely reconstructed.[6][8] Hudler in particular argues that because the ring 
lacks secure excavation records and entered collections through an unregulated 
acquisition history, its archaeological properties remain unclear and its 
evidentiary value must be treated with caution.[8]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 
'source_ids': ['20260628161526223570_993ff599', '20260628161526260710_56437a35',
'20260628161526299320_cea5570f', '20260628161526340237_391a31d0']}, {'text': 
'Contrasting with Evans’s presentation of the ring as part of a treasure from 
Thisbê, later catalogues associate the Ring of Nestor with the broader Pylos 
region and with finds from Kakovatos in Messenia.[16] A catalog-style entry in a
chapter on "Minoan glyptic" lists the ring as a gold signet ring from Kakovatos,
Messenia, giving its length as 3.27 cm, dating it to Late Minoan IB–II 
(1530–1400 BC), and identifying its current holding institution as the Ashmolean
Museum in Oxford.[16] This discrepancy between Evans’s Boeotian attribution and 
the later Kakovatos entry highlights the continuing uncertainty over the 
original findspot and underscores that the ring’s provenance is not securely 
established.[1][5][16]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526223570_993ff599', 
'20260628161533948547_acd64085']}, {'text': 'Because of these conflicting 
geographic and contextual associations, many recent authors emphasise that the 
ring should not be treated as a securely provenanced artefact tied to the Palace
of Nestor excavations at Pylos, even though it is often discussed in the same 
regional framework.[5][6][9] Davis notes that the ring may not have originated 
from the palace site itself and urges that earlier assumptions linking it 
directly to that excavation be reconsidered.[5][6] A technological study of gold
objects from Pylian tholos tombs similarly cautions that the Ring of Nestor, 
lacking firm provenance, should not be used on an equal footing with securely 
excavated material when drawing conclusions about local production and 
deposition practices.[9]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526223570_993ff599', '20260628161526260710_56437a35', 
'20260628161526384198_063ab3c8']}]}, {'title': 'Description', 'section_type': 
'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628161537039428_0e9a8710'], 
'blocks': [{'text': 'Evans described the Ring of Nestor as a large gold signet 
ring constructed from gold sheet, with a substantial hoop supporting an 
oversized bezel that carries the engraved imagery.[1][2] In his technical 
discussion, he recorded measurements and manufacturing notes for the group of 
rings from the supposed Thisbê treasure, identifying the Ring of Nestor as 
distinguished by its unusually large size and by the complexity of its glyptic 
decoration compared with the other pieces.[3] Subsequent catalogues, including 
the Cambridge "Minoan glyptic" chapter, give a length of approximately 3.27 cm 
for the ring, confirming its status as one of the largest known Aegean signet 
rings.[16]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526116364_0b859111', 
'20260628161526151885_5767b64e', '20260628161533948547_acd64085']}, {'text': 
'The ring’s material is consistently identified as gold, and it has been treated
in scholarship as a key example of Aegean gold glyptic and of the technical 
skill involved in producing sheet-gold rings with elaborately engraved 
bezels.[1][5][9] Evans emphasised the quality of the workmanship and regarded 
the assemblage that included the Ring of Nestor as a "truly royal treasure" on 
account of its rich gold content and the sophistication of its engraving.[2] 
Later technological studies of Mycenaean gold objects from Pylos include the 
Ring of Nestor among the comparative examples used to discuss alloy composition,
construction techniques, and possible workshop traditions, although they stress 
that its uncertain provenance limits its use as primary evidence.[9]', 'kind': 
'paragraph', 'source_ids': ['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', 
'20260628161526116364_0b859111', '20260628161526223570_993ff599', 
'20260628161526384198_063ab3c8']}, {'text': 'Modern museum cataloguing provides 
additional descriptive information and chronological attributions. The Ashmolean
Museum’s image library lists an entry "AN1938.1130 – Signet ring" with the 
preferred descriptive title "The Ring of Nestor" and assigns it to the Late 
Minoan I period, with a date range c. 1600–1450 BC.[10] The same ring is cited 
in the Cambridge glyptic catalog as "Oxford, Ashmolean Museum 1938–1130," dating
it to Late Minoan IB–II (1530–1400 BC), showing slight differences in the 
phrasing of the inventory number and in the detailed chronological 
subdivision.[16] Taken together, these museum and scholarly catalogues present 
the Ring of Nestor as a gold signet ring of the late 2nd millennium BC, with 
minor variation in the proposed dating within the broader Late Minoan 
sequence.[10][15][16]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161533656509_d7512d4b', '20260628161533948547_acd64085', 
'20260628161533913859_f729e462']}]}, {'title': 'Iconography and 
interpretations', 'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 
'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is notable for the exceptional richness 
of its engraved imagery. Evans described the bezel as bearing multiple elaborate
figured scenes that include anthropomorphic figures, animals, architectural 
structures, and elements of the landscape or sky.[1][2][3] Subsequent 
iconographic summaries highlight that the ring’s scene includes fourteen human 
figures and several fantastic creatures, making it one of the most densely 
populated compositions among known Aegean signet rings and surpassing other 
multi-figure examples in the glyptic corpus.[11] Such complexity has contributed
to the ring’s prominence in discussions of Aegean iconography and its use as a 
comparative piece in analysing other signet rings from Mycenaean 
graves.[5][6][9][11]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526116364_0b859111', 
'20260628161526151885_5767b64e', '20260628161533760498_43401e75', 
'20260628161526223570_993ff599', '20260628161526260710_56437a35', 
'20260628161526384198_063ab3c8']}, {'text': 'In his original interpretation, 
Evans saw the imagery of the Ring of Nestor as presenting processional and 
ritual scenes involving possible deities and worshippers, alongside symbolic 
motifs such as trees, altars, and celestial elements.[1][3][4] He argued that 
these scenes offered a vision of the "Minoan after-world," suggesting that the 
ring reflected belief in an afterlife and in the continued existence of the 
soul, thus providing rare visual testimony for religious conceptions in the 
Aegean Bronze Age.[1][3][4] Evans’s reading tied the ring closely to his broader
reconstruction of Minoan religion and was influential in early twentieth-century
interpretations of Aegean cult imagery.[1][3]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 
'source_ids': ['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526116364_0b859111',
'20260628161526151885_5767b64e', '20260628161526185536_e9f11b73']}, {'text': 
'Later scholarship has revisited the iconography from different perspectives, 
sometimes emphasising hybrid and "pseudo-Minoan" features. Studies of the ring’s
imagery note the presence of motifs such as incurved altars and combinations of 
figures and creatures that recall Egyptian iconography, arguing that the ring’s 
design incorporates Egyptian and hybrid elements rather than being purely 
Minoan.[12][13] One article describes the ring as "pseudo-Minoan" and explores 
its Egyptian iconography, using the case to address the broader issue of 
forgeries and authenticity in Aegean glyptic.[13] Another contribution focuses 
on contradictions in technical analyses and the importance of iconographic study
in assessing authenticity, discussing how features like incurved altars both 
support the ring’s plausibility and allow for the possibility that its imagery 
reflects cultural and artistic practices of the 1920s rather than the Bronze 
Age.[12]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': ['20260628161533793604_6869b567', 
'20260628161533839688_09b87a74']}, {'text': 'Recent interpretations also place 
the Ring of Nestor alongside other famous gold signet rings, such as the 
so-called Ring of Minos and rings from Tholos Tomb IV at Pylos, to investigate 
workshop traditions and stylistic phases in Mycenaean glyptic.[6][7][9] 
Comparative studies note that the band and bezel of the Ring of Nestor are 
strikingly similar to certain rings from securely excavated contexts, and use 
these parallels to argue for a particular phase of development and for 
connections between elite burial assemblages and high-status signet 
rings.[6][7][9] At the same time, scholars emphasise that the ring’s uncertain 
provenance and contested authenticity mean that its iconography should be used 
primarily in broader stylistic and technological discussions, rather than as 
decisive evidence for specific ritual or historical reconstructions.[7][8]', 
'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': ['20260628161526260710_56437a35', 
'20260628161526299320_cea5570f', '20260628161526384198_063ab3c8', 
'20260628161526340237_391a31d0']}]}, {'title': 'Scholarly debate', 
'section_type': 'section', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628161531756178_91ae4f62', '20260628161537039428_0e9a8710', 
'20260628161537144810_9aa50af4'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'Since its publication, 
the Ring of Nestor has been the subject of sustained scholarly debate concerning
its dating, provenance, and authenticity. Evans dated the ring to Late Helladic 
or Late Mycenaean times, observing pronounced Minoan stylistic elements in its 
glyptic style and religious imagery, and thus seeing it as evidence for Minoan 
influence in mainland Greece.[1][2] Subsequent museum and catalog attributions 
generally place the ring in the Late Minoan I–IB–II sequence, with the Ashmolean
Museum assigning a Late Minoan I date (c. 1600–1450 BC) and the Cambridge 
"Minoan glyptic" chapter suggesting Late Minoan IB–II (1530–1400 
BC).[10][15][16] These minor chronological differences reflect the broader 
complexities of Aegean Bronze Age chronology and the difficulties of dating an 
object without secure archaeological context.[5][8][9]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 
'source_ids': ['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526116364_0b859111',
'20260628161533656509_d7512d4b', '20260628161533913859_f729e462', 
'20260628161533948547_acd64085', '20260628161526223570_993ff599', 
'20260628161526340237_391a31d0', '20260628161526384198_063ab3c8']}, {'text': 
'Questions about the ring’s authenticity emerged in the decades following 
Evans’s publication. An article on the "Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring" notes that 
the ring was found in 1924 and accepted as authentic by Evans, whereas many 
other scholars condemned it as a forgery.[13] A review of the Corpus der 
minoischen und mykenischen Siegel remarks that while some researchers continue 
to harbour doubts about the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor, it is 
nonetheless included as item no. 277 in the corpus, alongside other suspect 
pieces.[14] A more recent study on "The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity" analyses contradictions in technical assessments and the role of 
iconography in evaluating such objects, highlighting how generations of scholars
have debated the genuineness of both the Nestor and Minos rings.[11][12][14]', 
'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': ['20260628161533839688_09b87a74', 
'20260628161533878559_a86a2a21', '20260628161533760498_43401e75', 
'20260628161533793604_6869b567']}, {'text': 'The ring’s provenance has also been
contested. Evans’s claim that it formed part of a sepulchral treasure from a 
Mycenaean tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia is based on information from local 
informants and cannot be independently verified.[1][2][3] Later scholarship 
associates the ring with Kakovatos in Messenia and with the Pylos region more 
broadly, using it in comparative discussions of Mycenaean graves and palace 
contexts.[5][16] These differing geographic attributions demonstrate that the 
original findspot is uncertain and that the ring’s association with specific 
sites, whether Thisbê or Kakovatos, remains debated.[5][8][16] Scholars such as 
Davis therefore advise that the ring not be treated as securely derived from the
Palace of Nestor excavations or from any particular tomb complex.[5][6]', 
'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': ['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', 
'20260628161526116364_0b859111', '20260628161526151885_5767b64e', 
'20260628161526223570_993ff599', '20260628161526260710_56437a35', 
'20260628161526340237_391a31d0', '20260628161533948547_acd64085']}, {'text': 
'Beyond authenticity and provenance, debate has focused on how the Ring of 
Nestor should be used in reconstructions of Aegean religion and society. Hudler 
argues that objects with insecure contexts, such as the Ring of Nestor, can 
allow arguments about Mycenaean beliefs, chronology, or social practices to 
"escape" methodological rigor, because their archaeological properties are 
difficult to verify.[8] She recommends that such artefacts be explicitly 
labelled as problematic and that their role be limited to broader stylistic or 
technological comparisons, rather than detailed historical scenarios.[8] Davis 
and colleagues likewise caution against relying on the ring as primary evidence 
for specific Pylian rituals or depositional practices, even while acknowledging 
its significance as a remarkable example of Aegean glyptic and a longstanding 
point of reference in scholarship.[6][7][9]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids':
['20260628161526340237_391a31d0', '20260628161526260710_56437a35', 
'20260628161526299320_cea5570f', '20260628161526384198_063ab3c8']}]}, {'title': 
'Museum collection and legacy', 'section_type': 'section', 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628161537287168_d27309bd'], 'blocks': [{'text':
'The Ring of Nestor entered the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford in the early 
twentieth century, following its acquisition and publication by Arthur 
Evans.[1][4] Museum cataloguing lists the ring under the inventory number 
AN1938.1130 and describes it as a signet ring with the preferred descriptive 
title "The Ring of Nestor," indicating that the name originally coined by Evans 
has become the standard designation in the collection.[10] Scholarly catalogues 
referring to the Ashmolean holdings use a slightly different formatting of the 
number, citing the ring as "Oxford, Ashmolean Museum 1938–1130," but clearly 
identify it as the same object.[16]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526068537_afd24e74', '20260628161526185536_e9f11b73', 
'20260628161533656509_d7512d4b', '20260628161533948547_acd64085']}, {'text': 
'Within the Ashmolean and in wider scholarship, the ring is frequently 
highlighted as a famous or celebrated piece of Aegean glyptic, despite the 
ongoing debates over its authenticity and provenance.[5][9][11][14] Discussions 
of Mycenaean gold rings at Pylos and of elite burial assemblages regularly cite 
the Ring of Nestor as a comparative example when analysing technical features, 
iconography, and workshop traditions.[6][7][9] The ring’s inclusion in the 
Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel and its appearance in numerous 
studies of Aegean iconography demonstrate its continued importance as a 
reference point for understanding the development of complex figural scenes on 
signet rings in the Bronze Age Aegean.[11][14][16]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 
'source_ids': ['20260628161526223570_993ff599', '20260628161526384198_063ab3c8',
'20260628161533760498_43401e75', '20260628161533878559_a86a2a21', 
'20260628161526260710_56437a35', '20260628161526299320_cea5570f', 
'20260628161533948547_acd64085']}, {'text': 'The discovery of the Griffin 
Warrior tomb at Pylos, with its rich assemblage of gold signet rings, has 
provided new securely excavated material against which the Ring of Nestor can be
compared.[5][6] Davis notes that before this discovery, the Ring of Nestor was 
the best-known large, elaborately engraved gold signet ring associated with the 
Pylos area and served as a key comparative piece in discussions of Mycenaean 
rings.[5] Subsequent work on the Griffin Warrior rings has allowed scholars to 
examine the technical and stylistic relationships between firmly provenanced 
objects and older finds like the Ring of Nestor, thereby refining assessments of
workshop traditions and the place of such celebrated but problematic pieces in 
the study of Aegean art.[6][9]', 'kind': 'paragraph', 'source_ids': 
['20260628161526223570_993ff599', '20260628161526260710_56437a35', 
'20260628161526384198_063ab3c8']}]}, {'title': 'References', 'section_type': 
