   1938–1130. I. Pini, OJA 17, 1998, 3 ...” [16]                                
 • The object known as the Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring, reportedly     
   found in 1924 in a tomb assemblage at Kakovatos in Messenia/Thisbê–Boeotia   
   context, now in the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford, under inventory number         
   AN1938.1130 (also cited as 1938–1130). [4][10][13][16]                       
 • The Ring of Nestor is described in modern scholarship and museum catalogues  
   as a gold signet ring of Late Minoan I–IB–II date, with proposed absolute    
   dates ranging approximately from 1600 to 1400 BC. [10][15][16]               
 • Technical descriptions in the Cambridge 'Minoan Glyptic' chapter record that 
   the Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring measuring approximately 3.27 cm in  
   length. [16]                                                                 
 • Iconographic descriptions report that the Ring of Nestor’s bezel scene       
   includes 14 human figures and several fantastic creatures, making it one of  
   the most densely populated multi-figure compositions known among Aegean      
   signet rings. [11]                                                           
 • Scholarly and museum descriptions treat the Ring of Nestor’s imagery as      
   'pseudo-Minoan' and emphasize Egyptian and hybrid iconographic elements,     
   including motifs such as incurved altars that have been used in debates over 
   the ring’s authenticity. [12][13]                                            
 • The Ring of Nestor has been the subject of a long-standing authenticity      
   debate: Arthur J. Evans accepted it as genuine, but many scholars condemned  
   it as a forgery; despite persistent doubts noted in later reviews, the ring  
   is nonetheless included as no. 277 in the Corpus der minoischen und          
   mykenischen Siegel. [11][12][13][14]                                         

